% Metainfo: Parsing .torrent Files
% Chapter 2 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Metainfo Files}

When you download a torrent, you don't start with the actual content---you start
with a small \texttt{.torrent} file, typically just a few kilobytes. This file,
formally called a \emph{metainfo} file, is the blueprint for the entire download.
It tells your client where to find other peers, what files you're downloading,
how large they are, and crucially, how to verify that every byte you receive
is authentic.

The metainfo file is bencoded, making our decoder from the previous chapter the
foundation for this one. Understanding the metainfo structure is essential because
it defines the ``contract'' between all peers sharing a torrent. Every client
that joins the swarm must interpret this file identically, or the swarm fragments.

\subsection{Why Metainfo Matters}

Before examining the structure, consider what problems the metainfo file solves:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Discovery}: How do peers find each other? The metainfo contains
  the tracker URL---a coordination server that maintains a list of peers.

\item \textbf{Identity}: How do we know two clients are sharing the same content?
  The \emph{info hash}---a SHA1 hash of part of the metainfo---serves as a
  globally unique identifier. Clients with the same info hash are sharing the
  same torrent.

\item \textbf{Integrity}: How do we verify downloaded data isn't corrupted or
  malicious? The metainfo contains SHA1 hashes of every piece. Each downloaded
  piece is hashed and compared; mismatches are rejected and re-downloaded.

\item \textbf{Structure}: What exactly are we downloading? The metainfo describes
  the files---their names, sizes, and how they map to pieces.
\end{enumerate}

These four concerns---discovery, identity, integrity, and structure---are all
encoded in a single bencoded dictionary.

\subsection{Top-Level Structure}

At the top level, a metainfo file is a bencoded dictionary. The BitTorrent
Enhancement Proposal 3 (BEP 3) defines two required keys:

\begin{description}
\item[announce] A UTF-8 string containing the tracker's announce URL. This is
  where peers register themselves and discover other peers. Common protocols
  are HTTP (\texttt{http://...}) and UDP (\texttt{udp://...}).

\item[info] A dictionary containing all metadata about the torrent's content.
  This includes file names, sizes, piece length, and the concatenated SHA1
  hashes of all pieces. The \texttt{info} dictionary is special---more on
  this shortly.
\end{description}

Optional keys include \texttt{announce-list} (for multiple trackers),
\texttt{creation date}, \texttt{comment}, \texttt{created by}, and
\texttt{encoding}. Our implementation focuses on the required fields;
optional fields can be added later without changing the core parsing logic.

\subsection{The Info Dictionary: Heart of the Torrent}

The \texttt{info} dictionary is the most important part of the metainfo file.
It contains everything needed to download and verify the content:

\begin{description}
\item[name] A UTF-8 string suggesting a filename (for single-file torrents) or
  directory name (for multi-file torrents). Clients may use this as the default
  save location, though users can override it.

\item[piece length] An integer specifying the size of each piece in bytes.
  Pieces are the fundamental unit of transfer and verification in BitTorrent.
  Common values are powers of two: 256 KiB (262144), 512 KiB, 1 MiB, 2 MiB,
  or 4 MiB. Larger pieces mean fewer hashes in the metainfo but slower
  verification of individual chunks.

\item[pieces] A byte string containing the concatenated SHA1 hashes of all
  pieces. Since SHA1 produces 20-byte hashes, this string's length is always
  a multiple of 20. The first 20 bytes are the hash of piece 0, the next 20
  are the hash of piece 1, and so on.

\item[length] (single-file only) The total size of the file in bytes.

\item[files] (multi-file only) A list of dictionaries, each describing one
  file in the torrent.
\end{description}

The presence of \texttt{length} indicates a single-file torrent; the presence
of \texttt{files} indicates a multi-file torrent. These are mutually exclusive.

\subsection{The Info Hash: Identity of a Torrent}

The \texttt{info} dictionary has a unique property: its exact bencoded byte
representation is hashed with SHA1 to produce the \emph{info hash}. This 20-byte
value is the torrent's identity---its fingerprint in the BitTorrent network.

\subsubsection{Why the Info Hash Matters}

The info hash serves three critical purposes:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Peer Discovery}: When contacting a tracker, clients send their
  info hash. The tracker uses this to group peers---only clients with matching
  info hashes are introduced to each other. Without this, you might connect to
  peers sharing completely different content.

\item \textbf{Handshake Verification}: The first message peers exchange includes
  the info hash. If the hashes don't match, the connection is immediately closed.
  This prevents protocol-level mix-ups before any data is transferred.

\item \textbf{Content Addressing}: The info hash is essentially a content address.
  Magnet links---those \texttt{magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...} URLs---are just info hashes
  in disguise. Given only the info hash, a client can find peers, retrieve the
  full metainfo from them, and begin downloading.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Immutability: The Defining Property}

The info hash is \emph{immutable}---it cannot be changed without creating a
different torrent. This follows directly from how it's computed:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Take the \texttt{info} dictionary from the metainfo file.
\item Encode it to bencode bytes (using canonical encoding with sorted keys).
\item Compute the SHA1 hash of those bytes.
\end{enumerate}

Any modification to the info dictionary---changing a filename, altering piece
length, adding a file---produces a different SHA1 hash. The result is a different
info hash, which means a different torrent. Peers sharing the original content
will not recognize the modified torrent as the same.

This immutability is a feature, not a limitation:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Tamper Evidence}: If someone modifies the metainfo (to inject
  malware, change filenames maliciously, etc.), the info hash changes. Users
  who obtained the original info hash can detect the tampering.

\item \textbf{Caching}: Since the same info hash always refers to the same
  content, clients can cache piece data indefinitely without revalidation.

\item \textbf{Deduplication}: If two torrents have the same info hash, they
  contain identical content. This enables swarm merging and cross-seeding.
\end{itemize}

Note that the \texttt{announce} URL is \emph{not} part of the info dictionary,
so changing trackers doesn't change the info hash. This is intentional---the
same content can be tracked by multiple trackers without fragmenting the swarm.

\subsubsection{Computing the Info Hash}

Computing the info hash requires care. We must re-encode the info dictionary
to canonical bencode bytes, not simply extract the raw bytes from the original
file. While extracting raw bytes would work for well-formed torrents, re-encoding
guarantees correctness regardless of how the original file was structured.

Our [[BTreeMap]]-based [[BencodeValue::Dict]] naturally produces sorted keys,
ensuring canonical encoding. The computation is straightforward: encode the
info dictionary, then hash the result with SHA1.

\subsection{Single-File vs Multi-File Torrents}

The info dictionary supports two mutually exclusive formats, distinguished by
which keys are present:

\subsubsection{Single-File Format}

A single-file torrent represents exactly one file. The info dictionary contains:

\begin{description}
\item[name] The suggested filename (e.g., \texttt{"ubuntu-24.04.iso"}).
\item[length] The file size in bytes.
\item[piece length] Bytes per piece.
\item[pieces] Concatenated SHA1 hashes.
\end{description}

For example, a torrent for a 3.3 GB Ubuntu ISO with 256 KiB pieces would have:
\begin{verbatim}
d
  6:length i3303444480e
  4:name 38:ubuntu-24.04.3-live-server-amd64.iso
  12:piece length i262144e
  6:pieces 252040:<12602 SHA1 hashes>
e
\end{verbatim}

The number of pieces is $\lceil \texttt{length} / \texttt{piece length} \rceil$.
For a 3.3 GB file with 256 KiB pieces: $\lceil 3303444480 / 262144 \rceil = 12602$
pieces, requiring $12602 \times 20 = 252040$ bytes for the piece hashes.

\subsubsection{Multi-File Format}

A multi-file torrent represents a directory containing multiple files. The
info dictionary contains:

\begin{description}
\item[name] The suggested directory name (e.g., \texttt{"album"}).
\item[files] A list of file entries, each with:
  \begin{description}
  \item[length] The file size in bytes.
  \item[path] A list of path components (e.g., \texttt{["cover", "front.jpg"]}).
  \end{description}
\item[piece length] Bytes per piece.
\item[pieces] Concatenated SHA1 hashes.
\end{description}

For example, a torrent containing an album might look like:
\begin{verbatim}
d
  5:files l
    d 6:length i5242880e 4:path l 9:track1.mp3 e e
    d 6:length i4194304e 4:path l 9:track2.mp3 e e
    d 6:length i1048576e 4:path l 5:cover 9:front.jpg e e
  e
  4:name 5:album
  12:piece length i262144e
  6:pieces ...<hashes>...
e
\end{verbatim}

The path for \texttt{front.jpg} is \texttt{["cover", "front.jpg"]}, which
resolves to \texttt{album/cover/front.jpg} when the torrent name is prepended.

\subsubsection{How Pieces Span Files}

In multi-file torrents, pieces are conceptually laid out across a single
contiguous byte stream formed by concatenating all files in order. A piece
may span file boundaries---the last bytes of one file and the first bytes
of the next might be in the same piece.

For example, if piece length is 1 MiB and file A is 1.5 MiB while file B is
0.5 MiB:
\begin{itemize}
\item Piece 0: bytes 0--1048575 of file A
\item Piece 1: bytes 1048576--1572863 of file A, then bytes 0--524287 of file B
\end{itemize}

This is why our [[Metainfo]] struct stores both individual [[FileInfo]] entries
and a [[total\_length]]---the total length determines piece boundaries.

\subsubsection{Unifying the Formats}

We unify these formats into a single representation: a torrent is always a list
of files, with single-file torrents being the degenerate case of a one-element
list. This simplifies our data structures and the code that uses them. Rather
than branching on ``is this single-file or multi-file?'' everywhere, consumers
can simply iterate over the file list.

\subsection{The FileInfo Structure}

Each file in a torrent is represented by its length and path. For single-file
torrents, the path is simply the filename. For multi-file torrents, the path
includes directory components.

<<file info struct>>=
/// Information about a single file within a torrent.
///
/// For single-file torrents, this represents the entire torrent content.
/// For multi-file torrents, each file in the torrent has its own `FileInfo`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FileInfo {
    /// Size of the file in bytes.
    pub length: u64,

    /// Path to the file, relative to the torrent's root directory.
    ///
    /// For single-file torrents, this is just the filename.
    /// For multi-file torrents, this includes directory components.
    pub path: PathBuf,
}
@

The path is stored as a [[PathBuf]] rather than a [[String]] because file paths
can contain arbitrary bytes in BitTorrent (the protocol uses byte strings, not
UTF-8 strings). [[PathBuf]] handles this correctly on most platforms.

\subsection{The Metainfo Structure}

The main [[Metainfo]] struct captures everything we need from a torrent file:

<<metainfo struct>>=
/// Parsed contents of a .torrent file.
///
/// This structure contains all the information needed to download a torrent:
/// - Where to find peers (tracker URL)
/// - How to identify the torrent (info hash)
/// - How to verify downloaded data (piece hashes)
/// - What files comprise the torrent (file list)
///
/// The structure unifies single-file and multi-file torrents into a consistent
/// representation where every torrent has a list of files (single-file torrents
/// simply have a one-element list).
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Metainfo {
    /// The tracker's announce URL.
    ///
    /// Peers register with this URL to discover other peers sharing the torrent.
    /// The URL typically uses HTTP or UDP protocol.
    pub announce: String,

    /// The 20-byte SHA1 hash of the bencoded info dictionary.
    ///
    /// This uniquely identifies the torrent across the BitTorrent network.
    /// All peers sharing the same content have the same info hash.
    pub info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Size of each piece in bytes.
    ///
    /// Pieces are the unit of data transfer and verification in BitTorrent.
    /// Typical values are powers of two between 256KB (262144) and 4MB (4194304).
    /// The last piece may be smaller than this value.
    pub piece_length: u32,

    /// SHA1 hashes of each piece, in order.
    ///
    /// After downloading a piece, its SHA1 hash is computed and compared to
    /// the corresponding entry here. A mismatch indicates corruption.
    pub piece_hashes: Vec<[u8; 20]>,

    /// Total size of the torrent content in bytes.
    ///
    /// This is the sum of all file lengths. For single-file torrents, this
    /// equals the file's length. For multi-file torrents, it's the total
    /// across all files.
    pub total_length: u64,

    /// List of files in the torrent.
    ///
    /// Single-file torrents have exactly one entry. Multi-file torrents have
    /// one entry per file. Files are stored in the order they appear in the
    /// metainfo, which determines how piece boundaries align with file content.
    pub files: Vec<FileInfo>,

    /// Name of the torrent.
    ///
    /// For single-file torrents, this is the filename.
    /// For multi-file torrents, this is the root directory name.
    pub name: String,
}
@

Several design decisions deserve explanation:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{info\_hash as fixed array}: We use \texttt{[u8; 20]} rather than
  \texttt{Vec<u8>} because SHA1 hashes are always exactly 20 bytes. The fixed
  size prevents accidental misuse and enables efficient comparison.

\item \textbf{piece\_length as u32}: Piece lengths are typically between 256KB
  and 4MB, well within u32 range. Using u32 saves memory and matches the
  protocol's 32-bit piece index.

\item \textbf{piece\_hashes as Vec}: The number of pieces varies widely---a small
  torrent might have 10 pieces, while a large one could have 100,000. A vector
  provides the flexibility needed.

\item \textbf{total\_length as u64}: Modern torrents can exceed 4GB, requiring
  64-bit file sizes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Error Handling}

Parsing metainfo can fail in several ways. We define a dedicated error type
to provide clear feedback about what went wrong.

<<metainfo errors>>=
/// Errors that can occur when parsing a metainfo file.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum MetainfoError {
    /// A required field is missing from the metainfo dictionary.
    #[error("missing required field: {0}")]
    MissingField(&'static str),

    /// The piece length is invalid (zero, negative, or too large).
    #[error("invalid piece length: {0}")]
    InvalidPieceLength(String),

    /// The pieces field is malformed (not a multiple of 20 bytes).
    #[error("invalid piece hashes: {0}")]
    InvalidPieceHashes(String),

    /// The multi-file torrent has an invalid files array.
    #[error("invalid multi-file torrent: {0}")]
    InvalidMultiFile(String),
}
@

These error variants cover the main parsing failure modes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{MissingField}: A required key (like ``announce'' or ``info'')
  is absent from the dictionary. The parameter identifies which field.

\item \textbf{InvalidPieceLength}: The piece length is zero (would cause
  division by zero), negative, or unreasonably large.

\item \textbf{InvalidPieceHashes}: The ``pieces'' field isn't a valid
  concatenation of 20-byte SHA1 hashes. This happens when its length
  isn't a multiple of 20.

\item \textbf{InvalidMultiFile}: The multi-file torrent's ``files'' array
  is malformed. This can happen when entries lack required fields, have
  invalid types, or contain empty paths.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Overview: From Bytes to Metainfo}

Before diving into the code, let's trace the parsing process from raw bytes to
a usable [[Metainfo]] structure. This walkthrough will help you understand how
the pieces fit together.

\subsubsection{Step 1: Bencode Decoding}

The input is raw bytes---the contents of a \texttt{.torrent} file. We first
decode these bytes using our bencode decoder from Chapter 1:

\begin{verbatim}
let value = decode(data)?;
\end{verbatim}

This gives us a [[BencodeValue]], which should be a dictionary. If decoding
fails (invalid bencode) or the result isn't a dictionary, we reject the file
immediately.

\subsubsection{Step 2: Extracting Top-Level Fields}

With the root dictionary in hand, we extract the required top-level fields:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{announce}: A UTF-8 string containing the tracker URL.
\item \textbf{info}: A dictionary containing all content metadata.
\end{enumerate}

We need the info dictionary in two forms: as a [[BencodeValue]] (for info hash
computation) and as a [[BTreeMap]] (for field extraction). Helper functions
like [[get\_string]], [[get\_value]], and [[get\_dict\_field]] handle the
type checking.

\subsubsection{Step 3: Parsing the Info Dictionary}

From the info dictionary, we extract:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{name}: The torrent/file/directory name (required).
\item \textbf{piece length}: Size of each piece in bytes (required, must be positive).
\item \textbf{pieces}: The concatenated SHA1 hashes (required, length must be
  a multiple of 20).
\item \textbf{length} or \textbf{files}: One of these must be present to indicate
  single-file or multi-file format.
\end{enumerate}

Each field is validated as it's extracted. Invalid piece lengths, malformed
piece hashes, or missing required fields trigger immediate errors.

\subsubsection{Step 4: File List Construction}

Based on whether \texttt{length} or \texttt{files} is present, we call either
[[parse\_single\_file]] or [[parse\_multi\_file]]. Both return a vector of
[[FileInfo]] and the total content length.

For single-file torrents, this is trivial---one [[FileInfo]] with the name and
length. For multi-file torrents, we iterate through the files array, building
paths by prepending the torrent name to each file's path components.

\subsubsection{Step 5: Info Hash Computation}

Finally, we compute the info hash by re-encoding the info dictionary to bencode
bytes and hashing with SHA1. The [[compute\_info\_hash]] function handles this:

\begin{verbatim}
let info_hash = compute_info_hash(info_value);
\end{verbatim}

The result is a 20-byte array that uniquely identifies this torrent.

\subsubsection{Step 6: Assembling the Result}

With all fields extracted and validated, we construct the [[Metainfo]] struct:

\begin{verbatim}
Ok(Metainfo {
    announce,
    info_hash,
    piece_length,
    piece_hashes,
    total_length,
    files,
    name,
})
\end{verbatim}

The caller receives either this fully-validated structure or a descriptive
error explaining what went wrong.

